changelog

Version 2.0 — W3C Verifiable Credentials format

This schema version is for W3C Verifiable Credential (VC) format documents ONLY.
It follows the W3C Verifiable Credentials Data Model 2.0 and is meant to be
issued / signed / verified with the TrustVC library (@trustvc/trustvc).

What changed from 1.0:
- The document is now a W3C Verifiable Credential. The top level carries the
  standard VC envelope: "@context", "type" (must include "VerifiableCredential"),
  "issuer", "validFrom" (replaces "issuedOn"), and "credentialSubject".
- All certificate-of-award data (name, description, recipient, award, signature)
  now lives inside "credentialSubject" instead of at the document root.
- The issuer is now the W3C "issuer" property (a DID/URL string, or an object
  with an "id"), replacing the OpenAttestation "issuers" array.
- The issuer keeps the contact fields the OpenAttestation issuers[] carried:
  "name" and "uen" (both required, alongside "id"), plus optional "url" and
  "email". "uen" was required on issuers[] in 1.0 and stays required. The old
  "did" is now "issuer.id". "name" was required in 1.0 too, via the
  OpenAttestation 2.0 base schema that 1.0 pulls in with allOf. The
  OpenAttestation-only fields "documentStore" and "certificateStore" are gone.
- "identityProof" — which that same base schema REQUIRED on every issuer — is
  replaced by "issuer.id". The DID *is* the identity proof: a DNS-TXT / DNS-DID
  identityProof bound an issuer to a domain, and did:web does the same thing by
  putting the domain in the DID and resolving the DID document over HTTPS from
  it; a DID-type identityProof named the issuer's public key, which is now the
  verificationMethod in that DID document (or, for did:key, the DID itself).
  The binding moved from a DNS TXT record to the DID method; it did not go
  away.
  The W3C VC v2 context marks "issuer" as @protected, so no scoped @context can
  be attached to it; context.json declares these as top-level terms instead.
- Optional W3C/TrustVC properties are supported: "validUntil", "credentialStatus",
  "credentialSchema", "renderMethod", "id" and "proof".
- "renderMethod" replaces the OpenAttestation "$template" and requires "id",
  "type" ("EMBEDDED_RENDERER") and "templateName".
- "credentialStatus" is a W3C VC Data Model 2.0 Bitstring Status List entry and
  requires "type" ("BitstringStatusListEntry"), "statusPurpose" ("revocation"
  or "suspension"), "statusListIndex" and "statusListCredential". Only the
  W3C VC 2.0 status type is accepted here.

Required fields — unchanged in substance from 1.0:
- The document requires "@context", "type", "issuer", "validFrom" and
  "credentialSubject". "validFrom" carries over the requirement that 1.0
  placed on "issuedOn".
- The credential "id" is NOT supplied by the issuer. 1.0 required an "id" at the
  document root; in a W3C VC the "id" is generated — TrustVC mints a urn:uuid
  while signing and rejects any document that already carries one. The schema
  enforces this: an unsigned credential (no "proof") must not have an "id", a
  signed one may. An issuer's own reference number therefore no longer belongs
  in "id".
- "credentialSubject" requires "type", "name", "recipient", "award" and
  "signature" — the same set 1.0 required, plus the subject "type". No field
  that was optional in 1.0 has become mandatory.
- "credentialSubject.type" must name the "CertificateOfAward" type. It is not merely
  descriptive: context.json defines every term inside that type-scoped
  @context, so a subject without it cannot be signed.

New field:
- "additionalData" (optional) is added. 1.0 had no freeform field, so this is a
  deliberate addition rather than a carry-over. It is "@type": "@json" in
  context.json, so its contents are carried verbatim and may be arbitrarily
  nested without declaring each field. It is where issuer-specific data now
  belongs — including the reference number that used to live in the document
  "id", which a W3C VC generates and no longer lets the issuer set.

Extra properties:
- Every object the schema describes sets "additionalProperties": false:
  "credentialSubject", "recipient", "issuer", "award" and "signature".
- This removes no capability. A W3C VC is canonicalised as an RDF graph, so a
  property that context.json does not define has no IRI and the credential
  cannot be signed — undeclared fields were already impossible to issue, they
  just failed at signing time instead of at validation. Closing the objects
  makes schema.json say what the format actually allows.
- 1.0 genuinely could carry arbitrary fields because OpenAttestation wrapped a
  JSON blob. That ended with the move to W3C VC, not with this schema change.
- The document root stays open: it is the W3C VC envelope, and closing it
  would reject standard VC properties this schema does not list.

IMPORTANT: This version is for W3C VC documents only. If you are NOT using the
W3C VC format (e.g. OpenAttestation v2 / OpenCerts verifiable documents), please
use an older version instead: certificate-of-awards/1.0.
